const HTML = "<p>I used to say it half-jokingly: people are cattle.</p>\n<p>It was a crude way of describing something I kept seeing everywhere: crowds moving together in the same direction, phrases repeated in unison by people who would not be able to tell you, if pressed, where the phrases had come from, articles shared widely and read by almost nobody who shared them.</p>\n<p>We echo headlines as if we had investigated the underlying facts ourselves.</p>\n<p>We cheer for policies we could not explain if somebody asked us to. Most of the time we are less interested in understanding what is in front of us than in staying aligned with the people who happen to be around us.</p>\n<p>When I first made the comparison, I was generally somewhere outside the herd. The people I meant accepted what they were told and resented anyone who complicated the journey, while I had apparently arrived at my own views without any of the same assistance. That is a flattering place from which to inspect other people&#39;s judgement.</p>\n<p>One evening, watching a film, I recognised a public figure in its female villain. The resemblance seemed unmistakable to me. The real person had been outspoken, had challenged views others wanted left alone, and had been described as reckless, dangerous and unstable. I had seen that persistence treated as evidence against them, and now I was watching a fictional version of someone I recognised offered up for the audience to hate.</p>\n<p>The film didn&#39;t have to argue against what that person had said. It could put the recognisable figure in the villain&#39;s role and let the story do the rest, with the pleasure of watching her defeated carrying an opinion I might have scrutinised more carefully if it had arrived in a political speech. I had sat down to be entertained, which was precisely why I found the resemblance disturbing.</p>\n<p>I began thinking about the judgements I already had available when I heard a person&#39;s name. Sometimes I would have needed to go looking for the reasons afterwards, because I knew what to think of them before I could explain why. Familiarity had done work I was crediting to my own examination.</p>\n<p>It isn&#39;t possible to investigate everything, so I have to rely on other people. But I can forget where that reliance ends and my own knowledge begins, especially once I have repeated a judgement in public and made somebody else feel foolish for questioning it. Reconsidering then means more than replacing an opinion. It means admitting that the person I mocked may have understood something I hadn&#39;t.</p>\n<p>Suppose I have helped support a policy which turns out to be harmful. I have defended it to friends, dismissed objections and treated disagreement as evidence that someone has been fooled. Now the people affected have to live with the decision, while I have to live with having supported it. Their loss may be a livelihood, freedom or health; mine may be the account of myself as someone who would never have helped bring such a thing about.</p>\n<p>That difference should make my embarrassment seem small. Instead, it can make me work harder to keep believing I was right. I can question the new evidence, attack whoever brings it or insist that the consequences would have been worse without the policy, and some of those arguments might deserve consideration. What matters is whether I am considering them to find out what happened, or because a particular answer has become intolerable to me.</p>\n<p>A label can save all that trouble. Call the objection misinformation and I may feel I have dealt with it, without needing to repeat the claim or explain what makes it false. Sometimes it is false. But the label cannot establish its own accuracy, and borrowing somebody else&#39;s refusal to examine a claim does not make me well-informed.</p>\n<p>Nor would reversing every popular opinion make me independent. Assuming the unpopular person must be right leaves my judgement just as dependent on the group, with the additional satisfaction of imagining myself too brave to join it. That would be a particularly convenient customer for anyone selling nonsense as forbidden knowledge.</p>\n<p>What frightens me about the slaughterhouse is the willingness to keep walking. The cattle don&#39;t need to understand the destination; familiar movement and the animals around them are enough. I used to enjoy the comparison because it made other people&#39;s obedience look stupid. I find less to enjoy when I consider how readily I accept reassurance from people I trust, how unpleasant I find the prospect of admitting a serious mistake, and how both can keep me moving after I have been given a reason to stop.</p>\n<p>By then, stopping may require an apology I don&#39;t want to make. Continuing may require someone else to bear the consequences of a decision I no longer have good grounds to defend. I would rather have the humiliating conversation while there is still something that can be changed.</p>\n";

